window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-06cfeb79b330c212"]={"key":"ny_main-06cfeb79b330c212","group":"ny_main","uid":"06cfeb79b330c212","rank":39,"title":"cinderhall everstead delivery operating plan","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are Jelena Skarsgard, the watch operations lieutenant for the Cinderhall Logistics Police Division, part of Maryland’s public freight authority. On Thursday morning the Cinderhall Logistics Board must approve police coverage for Everstead Industries’ single high-risk chemical delivery. The attached `everstead_delivery_source_packet.docx` contains the current correspondence, stakeholder notes, operating records, staffing alternatives, and the frozen Maryland legal and policy material as of 15 October 2025. Everstead’s latest client manifest lists eight vehicles, while the frozen police logistics sheet lists six. Rhodri Gauthier has also told Everstead that every vehicle will clear the gate without verification delays, although that assurance was informal. At the same time, the unit is halfway between radio check-ins and mobile dispatch, and some pilot users have begun treating a mobile “accepted” status as if it were gate clearance. Operations has not yet settled where the mandatory-safeguard boundary ends and preferred practice begins during that transition. Prepare an operating-plan presentation for the Board and the watch commanders, dispatchers, and gate personnel who will execute its decision, including personnel who have not worked with the mobile process. Compare the viable coverage and check-in alternatives, explain the material changes between the radio baseline and mobile pilot periods, make one clear recommendation, state which source governs the disputed vehicle count, and include a staffing plan that agrees with the recommendation. The selected procedure must work for Thursday’s transitional operation and after mobile dispatch becomes standard, while making the authorized change from current practice, ownership, timing, exceptions, and escalation clear enough to execute without calling you. Preserve a realistic service objective without turning the informal assurance into operating authority. Use only figures supported by the packet and apply its Maryland snapshot as of the stated date. Keep the presentation to no more than 10 slides, including any title or source slide. Write the completed file into the top level of the working directory using exactly the filename `everstead_delivery_operating_plan.pptx`.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/everstead_delivery_source_packet.docx` — Provides the correspondence, stakeholder notes, conflicting vehicle records, radio-baseline and mobile-pilot operating data, staffing alternatives, delivery constraints, and frozen Maryland legal and policy snapshot needed for the operating decision.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `everstead_delivery_operating_plan.pptx` — Board-ready operating-plan presentation containing the decision, alternative comparison, staffing plan, transitional and steady-state procedure, controls, and execution responsibilities for the delivery. (Place the file at the top level of the working directory.; Use exactly the required filename.; Use no more than 10 slides, including title and source material.; Make exactly one operating recommendation.; Use only factual figures supported by the supplied packet.; Cover both Thursday's transitional operation and the mobile-dispatch steady state.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/everstead_delivery_source_packet.docx","bytes":46943,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/fd73cb85fed0fced15d154cce06b28679a9fcdcf852ea9f3fc528e98a8b582bd.docx","sha256":"fd73cb85fed0fced15d154cce06b28679a9fcdcf852ea9f3fc528e98a8b582bd","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"EVERSTEAD INDUSTRIES\nThursday high-risk chemical delivery - police coverage decision packet\nDecision record\nCurrent packet detail\nBoard action\nCinderhall Logistics Board disposition requested by 05:15, Thursday 23 Oct 2025\nMovement\nHigh-risk chemical delivery to Cinderhall Logistics Freight Terminal, Gate 3 controlled area\nOperating window\nArrival and movement sequence 06:30-10:30; police coverage and closeout 05:45-12:15\nUsers\nWatch commanders, dispatch, Gate 3 personnel, escort/response personnel, and personnel new to mobile dispatch\nAuthority basis\nMaryland and Cinderhall local-policy snapshot frozen as of 15 Oct 2025\n\nPacket assembled for the Board decision. Operational records and authority extracts are frozen for this decision; this is not a live legal update.\n1. Dated delivery brief\nItem\nBrief note\nDelivery character\nSingle high-risk chemical movement. Vehicles are to arrive in sequence through Gate 3 and move only after controlled-entry and dispatch-record steps.\nService expectation\nEverstead requests orderly, low-delay processing. The delivery brief does not set an unconditional gate-time guarantee.\nFacility flow\nGate 3 has a controlled staging lane before the entry point. A vehicle with a record discrepancy is to remain in that lane while the record is resolved.\nBoard deadline\nThe Board must approve coverage before the 05:45 operational briefing. Board approval at 05:15 leaves time to issue the final roster and posts.\nPre-arrival control\nAt 05:45, the Watch Commander and Everstead Industries Dispatch reconcile Revision 3 into one final gate roster for Gate 3 and dispatch. A late change remains in staging until recorded supervisor resolution.\nOpening posture\nAt 06:15, the controlled staging lane and the accountable dispatch event record open for the 06:30-10:30 arrival sequence.\nCloseout\nDispatch and Gate 3 records close after the movement. Watch Operations after-action review is due Friday 24 Oct 2025 by 16:00.\n\n\n\n2. Current client manifest - Revision 3\nReceived from Everstead Industries Dispatch: 15 Oct 2025 16:20. Owner: Everstead Industries Dispatch. Revision 3 is the latest client planning communication in this packet.\nSeq.\nVehicle ID\nDriver record\nMovement ref\nPlanned arrival\nClient revision\n1\nEV-410\nED-441\nEVI-23-01\n06:30\nRev 3\n2\nEV-411\nED-442\nEVI-23-02\n06:45\nRev 3\n3\nEV-412\nED-443\nEVI-23-03\n07:00\nRev 3\n4\nEV-413\nED-444\nEVI-23-04\n07:20\nRev 3\n5\nEV-414\nED-445\nEVI-23-05\n07:40\nRev 3\n6\nEV-415\nED-446\nEVI-23-06\n08:00\nRev 3\n7\nEV-416\nED-447\nEVI-23-07\n08:20\nRev 3\n8\nEV-417\nED-448\nEVI-23-08\n08:40\nRev 3\n\nThe client revision is a capacity-planning communication. It is not a physical-entry record. Gate admission requires the final reconciled delivery record and positive vehicle/driver linkage at arrival.\n3. Frozen police logistics record - Revision 1\nCinderhall Logistics Police Division logistics sheet, frozen 13 Oct 2025 14:00. Owner: Watch Operations logistics desk. The frozen sheet was prepared before the current client Revision 3 was received.\nPolice Seq\nVeh ID\nDriver record\nGate lane\nLogistics rev\nPlanning status\n1\nEV-410\nED-441\nGate 3\nRev 1\nListed\n2\nEV-411\nED-442\nGate 3\nRev 1\nListed\n3\nEV-412\nED-443\nGate 3\nRev 1\nListed\n4\nEV-413\nED-444\nGate 3\nRev 1\nListed\n5\nEV-414\nED-445\nGate 3\nRev 1\nListed\n6\nEV-415\nED-446\nGate 3\nRev 1\nListed\n\nThe logistics sheet remains a police planning record until Watch Operations captures a later approved operational roster. It does not itself clear a vehicle, driver, or substitution.\n\n\n4. Correspondence and stakeholder notes\n14 Oct 2025 08:38 | From: Rhodri Gauthier | To: Everstead Industries Dispatch | Subject: Thursday Gate 3\nYou have my assurance that every vehicle will clear Gate 3 without verification delays. Keep the convoy moving and we will take care of the gate.\n14 Oct 2025 09:02 | From: Gate 3 post | To: Watch Operations\nThe current logistics sheet has six. A service assurance does not tell the post how to process a substitution, driver mismatch, or an eighth vehicle. The staging lane is available; the roadway is not a safe place to resolve a record issue.\n14 Oct 2025 11:40 | From: Dispatch desk | To: Watch Operations\nSome mobile-pilot users are reading Accepted as if it were gate clearance. At dispatch, Accepted means the report was received or assigned. Gate clearance still needs a separate verified event and a physical gate decision.\n14 Oct 2025 12:05 | From: Watch communications inbox | To: Gate 3 post | Subject: Fwd: Thursday Gate 3\n----- Forwarded message -----\nYou have my assurance that every vehicle will clear Gate 3 without verification delays. Keep the convoy moving and we will take care of the gate.\n\nSome mobile-pilot users are reading Accepted as if it were gate clearance. At dispatch, Accepted means the report was received or assigned. Gate clearance still needs a separate verified event and a physical gate decision.\n15 Oct 2025 16:20 | From: Everstead Industries Dispatch | To: Watch Operations\nRevision 3 lists eight vehicles. We need predictable flow but will use the controlled staging lane for any arrival that does not match the current record.\nStakeholder note\nRecorded concern\nCinderhall Logistics Board staff\nKeep a realistic service objective; do not make ordinary gate processing look avoidably slow.\nGate 3 post\nA vehicle, driver, or carrier substitution must not inherit a prior vehicle's place in the list.\nDispatch desk\nThursday will have mixed radio/mobile capability. Radio remains necessary for outage, failed acknowledgement, and personnel not trained on mobile.\nWatch Operations\nOne executable Thursday plan is required. The commander needs a clear exception decision and relief coverage without leaving verification unattended.\n\n\n\n5. Operating-record comparison\nFixed period boundaries for the exported Gate 3 records: radio baseline = 01 Sep 2025 00:00 through 14 Sep 2025 23:59; mobile pilot = 22 Sep 2025 00:00 through 05 Oct 2025 23:59. Use only delivery operations at Gate 3 for like-for-like comparison.\nRadio baseline operational event export\nEvent date\nRun ref\nUnit / veh\nCheckpoint\nCheck-in start\nDispatch status\nAck time\nPositive verify\nResult / issue\nChannel\nActivity\n01 Sep 2025\nRB-101\nR-101\nGate 3\n06:15\nAck\n06:22\n06:31\nVerified / released\nRadio\nDelivery\n03 Sep 2025\nRB-102\nR-102\nGate 3\n06:27\nradio receive\n06:36\n06:45\nVerified / released\nRDO\nDelivery\n05 Sep 2025\nRB-103\nR103\nGate 3\n06:35\nACK'D\n06:43\n06:54\nVerified / released\nradio-1\nDelivery\n08 Sep 2025\nRB-104\nR-104\nGate 3\n06:17\nAck\n06:25\n06:35\nHold - driver credential mismatch; resolved\nRadio\ndelivery\n10 Sep 2025\nRB-105\nR-105\nGate 3\n06:30\nReceived\n06:40\n06:49\nVerified / released\nRADIO\nDelivery\n12 Sep 2025\nRB-106\nR-106\nGate 3\n06:10\nradio receive\n06:17\n06:26\nVerified / released\nRDO\nDELIVERY\n12 Sep 2025\nRB-106\nR-106\nGate 3\n06:10\nradio receive\n06:17\n06:26\nVerified / released\nRDO\nDELIVERY\n13 Sep 2025\nRB-TRN\nTR-01\nGate 3\n09:00\nAck\n09:02\n09:04\nTraining road check\nRadio\nTraining\n14 Sep 2025\nRB-CAN\nR-107\nGate 3\n08:00\nAck\n08:02\n--\nCancelled before gate\nRadio\nCancelled\n14 Sep 2025\nRB-OTH\nO-1\nGate 1\n07:10\nAck\n07:13\n07:21\nRoutine facility record\nRadio\nUnrelated\n\nMobile-pilot operational event export\nEvent date\nRun ref\nUnit / veh\nCheckpoint\nCheck-in start\nDispatch status\nAck time\nPositive verify\nResult / issue\nChannel\nActivity\n22 Sep 2025\nMP-201\nM-201\nGate 3\n06:14\nAccepted\n06:17\n06:28\nVerified / released\nMobile\nDelivery\n24 Sep 2025\nMP-202\nM-202\nGate 3\n06:28\naccepted\n06:30\n06:44\nHold - vehicle tag format mismatch; corrected\nmbl\nDelivery\n24 Sep 2025\nMP-202\nM 202\nGate 3\n06:28\nAccepted\n06:30\n06:44\nHold - vehicle tag format mismatch; corrected\nmbl\ndelivery\n26 Sep 2025\nMP-203\nM 203\nG-3\n06:12\nAcknowledged\n06:15\n06:27\nVerified / released\nMobile app\nDelivery\n26 Sep 2025\nMP-203\nM 203\nG-3\n06:12\nAcknowledged\n06:15\n06:27\nVerified / released\nMobile app\nDelivery\n29 Sep 2025\nMP-204\nM-204\nGate 3\n06:31\nAccepted\n06:33\n06:48\nRework - gate treated Accepted as clearance; returned to verification\nMBL\ndelivery\n01 Oct 2025\nMP-205\nM-205\nGate 3\n06:17\nreceived\n06:20\n06:35\nConnectivity fallback to radio; verified / released\nMobile\nDelivery\n03 Oct 2025\nMP-206\nM-206\nGate 3\n06:23\naccepted\n06:25\n06:38\nHold - driver record mismatch; resolved\nmbl\nDelivery\n05 Oct 2025\nMP-207\nM-207\nGate 3\n07:1?\nAccepted\n07:18\n07:31\nVerified / released\nmobile\nDelivery\n30 Sep 2025\nMP-DEMO\nDEMO-2\nGate 3\n08:10\nAccepted\n08:12\n08:18\nDemonstration run\nMobile\nDemo\n04 Oct 2025\nMP-CAN\nM-208\nGate 3\n07:50\nAccepted\n07:52\n--\nCancelled before gate\nMobile\nCancelled\n02 Oct 2025\nMP-OTH\nZ-1\nGate 4\n09:10\nAccepted\n09:12\n09:24\nRoutine facility record\nMobile\nUnrelated\n\nExport columns use the labels produced by the two systems. 'Positive verify' is the time the gate record shows vehicle/driver linkage complete; a dispatch status is not a physical gate event.\n6. Thursday resources and coverage alternatives\nResource\nThursday availability / condition\nSworn police personnel\nSeven sworn personnel confirmed for 05:45-12:15. No additional sworn augmentation is available before 10:00. One voluntary overtime backfill is possible after 10:00 only if the Board approves it by 05:15.\nDispatch\nOne dispatcher is assigned at the dispatch console for the full 05:45-12:15 window. There is no second dispatcher available for a parallel log.\nVehicles and posts\nTwo marked police utility units are assigned for escort/response. Gate 3 and its controlled staging lane are available. No additional gate lane is opened.\nRelief\nA relief/reserve officer may cover a gate-verification or staging post during breaks and may be moved by the watch commander during an exception. The commander retains command and release authority.\nTransition\nMobile remains a pilot capability on Thursday. Trained users may use it; radio is required for continuity and for personnel who have not completed the mobile briefing.\n\nCoverage alternative\nVehicle basis\nSworn roles\nDispatch / communications\nTotal positions\nOperational condition\nA. Full-capacity hybrid\n8 planned\n1 commander; 2 gate verification; 1 staging/traffic; 2 escort/response; 1 relief/reserve\n1 dispatcher; mobile receipt/assignment plus radio parallel and fallback\n8 (7 sworn + 1 dispatcher)\nTwo gate verifiers can work while staging and relief remain available. No overtime needed.\nB. Six-vehicle base + verified surge\n6 base; 7th/8th staged until supervisor activates on-site reserve as second gate verifier\n1 commander; 1 gate verification; 1 staging/traffic; 2 escort/response; 1 relief/reserve\n1 dispatcher; mixed radio/mobile\n7 (6 sworn + 1 dispatcher)\nFeasible when arrivals stay sequenced. It preserves a surge path but delays the seventh/eighth vehicle while the reserve changes post.\nC. Lean mobile-dependent\n8 nominal\n1 commander; 1 combined gate/staging; 2 escort/response\n1 dispatcher; mobile used as principal arrival workflow; radio only after failure\n5 (4 sworn + 1 dispatcher)\nUses fewer people but has no relief or second verifier. A mismatch or mobile issue consumes the combined post.\n\n\n\n7. Current transition and frozen authority snapshot\nCurrent practice / terms\nRecord\nRadio baseline\nRadio check-in and dispatcher log are the current operational baseline. Gate staff report arrival, verification, gate action, and release as separate closed-loop events.\nThursday mobile use\nMobile may be used by trained personnel to send or receive an assignment. Dispatch must retain an accountable event record, and radio remains the fallback.\nMobile Accepted\nMeans dispatch receipt or assignment acknowledgement. It is not physical gate clearance, verification, entry approval, or movement release.\nWhen mobile becomes standard\nA future formal mobile-standard activation may make mobile the primary dispatch workflow. It does not change verification, controlled entry, supervisor exception, record, or release requirements.\n\nFrozen Maryland and local-policy snapshot - current as of 15 Oct 2025\nFrozen source\nOperational boundary\nMaryland Freight Facility Access Regulation (MFAR) 8.4 - Controlled entry\nBefore controlled freight-area entry, positively associate the arriving vehicle and driver with an authorized current delivery record.\nMFAR 8.6 - No implied waiver\nA carrier schedule, service assurance, escort, emergency equipment, or dispatch acknowledgement does not waive access-control or hazardous-material obligations.\nMFAR 8.7 - Exception resolution\nAn unlisted or substituted vehicle remains outside controlled entry pending supervisor decision and recorded resolution.\nCLPD General Order GO-4.12, Hazardous Freight Movements, sections 3, 5, 6\nUse controlled staging; release movement only after required checks and command authorization; for spill, security threat, or disabled vehicle stop the routine movement, notify command, and move to incident command.\nCLPD Communications Directive CD-2.7, Dispatch Event Accountability, sections 2-4\nKeep arrival, verification, gate clearance, and release distinct; Accepted is receipt/assignment only; use a radio fallback and reconcile it into the accountable dispatch record.\nGate Post Order G-3, paragraphs 4.2-4.5\nGate verifier records positive linkage; unresolved mismatch stays in controlled staging; a gate officer may not treat an informal assurance as clearance.\n\nMandatory: positive vehicle/driver linkage, controlled staging for unresolved records, supervisor exception decision, accountable dispatch record, clear release authority, and usable fallback. Preferred efficiency practices: advance roster reconciliation, scheduled arrival spacing, mobile-first convenience when trained, and queue updates. Preferred practices do not replace mandatory controls.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/everstead_delivery_operating_plan.pptx","bytes":73301,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/80223b6d229f4f3a5f3744fdf2899176dabb14f2e154b5771b585fcb9ff173ca.pptx","sha256":"80223b6d229f4f3a5f3744fdf2899176dabb14f2e154b5771b585fcb9ff173ca","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nCINDERHALL LOGISTICS BOARD | DECISION BRIEF\nAuthorize the Thursday Everstead high-risk chemical delivery operating plan\nFull-capacity hybrid serves current 8-vehicle capacity; positive verification is non-waivable.\nBOARD ACTION\nAuthorize Plan A - Full-capacity hybrid - for Thursday 23 Oct 2025; approve activation by 05:15.\nEvent\nEverstead delivery\nVenue\nGate 3 controlled area\nMovement window\n06:30-10:30\nCoverage / closeout\n05:45-12:15\nOperating audience\nBoard members; watch commanders; dispatch; Gate 3 personnel; escort/response personnel; and staff new to mobile dispatch.\nOperational owner: Jelena Skarsgard, Watch Operations Lieutenant.\nGoverning frame\nMaryland jurisdiction\nMaryland and Cinderhall local-policy snapshot frozen as of 15 Oct 2025\nDecision purpose: authorize an executable delivery operation, not a later study. This deck gives the Board and posts one operating model and one control boundary.\nMaryland snapshot frozen as of 15 Oct 2025\n1\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\nDECISION BASIS\nCapacity planning has one source; admission has a separate authority\n02\nCURRENT CLIENT RECORD\n8 vehicles\nEverstead client manifest, Revision 3\nReceived from Everstead Industries Dispatch: 15 Oct 2025 16:20. Owner: Everstead Industries Dispatch.\nFROZEN POLICE RECORD\n6 vehicles\nCLPD logistics sheet, Revision 1\nCinderhall Logistics Police Division logistics sheet, frozen 13 Oct 2025 14:00. Owner: Watch Operations logistics desk. The frozen sheet was prepared before the current client Revision 3 was received.\nAUTHORIZED PLANNING RULE\nPlan capacity to the current eight-vehicle client manifest.\nRevision currency and client ownership avoid under-planning. The older police sheet stays in the reconciliation file.\nPlanning capacity does not grant entry\nAt 05:45, the Watch Commander and Everstead Dispatch reconcile Revision 3 into the final gate roster. Each arriving vehicle and driver must still positively link to the authorized current delivery record before Gate Cleared or Released status. A planned eighth vehicle is not automatically admitted.\nInformal assurance\nRhodri Gauthier's no-delay assurance is not operational authority to waive verification.\nService objective: orderly staged arrivals and the fastest practical verified processing - not zero delay at any cost.\nRecord discipline\nThe forwarded 14 Oct chain is one correspondence source, not independent corroboration. Revision 3 is the operative planning revision; the reconciled gate roster controls an individual arrival.\nPacket refs: client manifest Rev 3; frozen CLPD logistics Rev 1; correspondence; Gate 3 record\n2\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\nOPTIONS COMPARED\nAuthorize the full-capacity hybrid; preserve verified flow through the transition\n03\nMODEL\nCAPACITY / STAFFING\nCHECK-IN MODEL\nCONTROL + SERVICE EFFECT\nTHURSDAY / STEADY STATE\nSELECTED\nFull-capacity hybrid\n8 planned\n8 (7 sworn + 1 dispatcher)\n1 dispatcher; mobile receipt/assignment plus radio parallel and fallback\nTwo gate verifiers can work while staging and relief remain available. No overtime needed.\nThursday: mobile + radio. Later: mobile primary, radio fallback; control boundary unchanged.\nVIABLE, NOT SELECTED\nSix-vehicle base + verified surge\n6 base; 7th/8th staged until supervisor activates on-site reserve as second gate verifier\n7 (6 sworn + 1 dispatcher)\n1 dispatcher; mixed radio/mobile\nFeasible when arrivals stay sequenced. It preserves a surge path but delays the seventh/eighth vehicle while the reserve changes post.\nThursday workable, but a seventh/eighth waits for post change. Later remains a surge model.\nDO NOT AUTHORIZE\nLean mobile-dependent\n8 nominal\n5 (4 sworn + 1 dispatcher)\n1 dispatcher; mobile used as principal arrival workflow; radio only after failure\nUses fewer people but has no relief or second verifier. A mismatch or mobile issue consumes the combined post.\nFast on paper, but depends on mobile interpretation and a combined post during a known transition risk.\nWhy the lean mobile-dependent model is rejected: the pilot's 4/6 exception/rework count includes accepted-as-clearance rework and connectivity fallback; it also has no relief or second verifier. The six-vehicle surge model is viable, but full eight-vehicle staffing avoids holding verified added vehicles for a post conversion.\nPacket refs: Thursday resources and coverage alternatives; operating-record comparison\n3\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\nTRANSITION EVIDENCE\nMobile improves timeliness, but the pilot does not prove a mobile-only control model\n04\nRADIO BASELINE\n01 Sep 2025 00:00 through 14 Sep 2025 23:59 | n=6 eligible arrivals\nMOBILE PILOT\n22 Sep 2025 00:00 through 05 Oct 2025 23:59 | n=6 eligible arrivals\nINTERPRETATION\nUse mobile for faster dispatch acknowledgement, while retaining separate verification, gate, release, and fallback controls.\nMedian dispatch acknowledgement\nSame metric definition in both periods\nRadio\n8.0min\nMobile\n2.5min\nLower is faster\nMedian check-in to positive verification\nSame metric definition in both periods\nRadio\n18.0min\nMobile\n15.5min\nLower is faster\nException / mismatch / rework\nSame metric definition in both periods\nRadio\n17.0%\nMobile\n67.0%\n1/6 vs 4/6 eligible events\nCleaning treatment and limitation\nNormalized vehicle, checkpoint, channel, and acknowledgement labels; removed 1 radio and 2 mobile duplicate/near-duplicate rows; excluded training/demo/cancelled/unrelated rows and the one malformed 07:1? start. Check-in = start through positive verification. Accepted alone is not an exception or clearance. Small pilot workflow and data-quality limits prevent inferring full steady-state safety.\nPacket refs: radio baseline and mobile-pilot event exports; derived after stated cleaning treatment\n4\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\nCONTROL BOUNDARY\nMandatory access-control steps stay separate from preferred efficiency practices\n05\nMANDATORY - no waiver by service assurance, escort, emergency equipment, or Accepted status\nPREFERRED - use when feasible; never a substitute for a required step\nACCEPTED\nDispatch received or assigned the report. NOT gate clearance, verification, entry approval, or movement release.\nVERIFIED\nGate verifier positively links vehicle + driver to the authorized current delivery record.\nGATE CLEARED\nGate officer permits controlled entry only after VERIFIED and the dispatch record is updated.\nRELEASED\nWatch Commander authorizes escort / movement only after all required checks are complete.\nHOLD\nKeep vehicle in controlled staging; dispatcher logs it; commander resolves mismatch before any entry or release.\nEvery time\n- Positive vehicle/driver linkage before controlled entry\n- Controlled staging for unresolved records\n- Supervisor decision for a mismatch or substitution\n- One accountable dispatch record\n- Radio fallback and record reconciliation\nUse when feasible\n- Advance roster reconciliation\n- Spaced arrivals and queue updates\n- Mobile-first convenience once trained\n- Service-oriented staging management\nClosed-loop rule\nSender reports status; dispatcher repeats and records it; the role owning the next status confirms its own action.\nAuthority: MFAR 8.4, 8.6, 8.7; CLPD GO-4.12; CD-2.7; Gate Post Order G-3 | frozen 15 Oct 2025\n5\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\nAUTHORIZED STAFFING\nPlan A uses the confirmed full-capacity hybrid: eight positions from briefing through closeout\n06\n8 (7 sworn + 1 dispatcher) | 8 planned | 05:45-12:15 | two marked police utility units | no overtime required\n1\nWatch Commander\nException decisions; movement release; keeps command authority\n2\nGate verification\nPositive vehicle/driver/current-record link; issue VERIFIED and GATE CLEARED\n1\nStaging / traffic\nProtect controlled queue and keep discrepancy work off the roadway\n2\nEscort / response\nOperate the two marked police utility units; escort and initial response\n1\nRelief / reserve\nRelieve gate/staging; backfill a key post during an exception\n1\nDispatch coordinator\nAccountable event log; mobile/radio acknowledgement; fallback record\nBreak / loss-of-post continuity\nDuring breaks, relief/reserve covers Gate or staging before a break. If a key post is lost, commander freezes new Gate Cleared / Released actions, stages arrivals, and backfills with relief/reserve.\nResource check\nMatches packet availability: seven sworn, one dispatcher, two utility units, one Gate 3/staging lane. The commander is not counted as a verifier; dispatch is not duplicated.\nPacket refs: Thursday resources and coverage alternatives; current delivery record and Gate 3 posts\n6\n\n=== 幻灯片 7 ===\nTHURSDAY PROCEDURE\nPre-arrival reconciliation and controlled arrival sequence\n07\n05:15\nBOARD AUTHORIZATION\nBoard approves Plan A. Jelena Skarsgard activates the plan and posts the briefing.\n05:45\nRECONCILE\nWatch Commander + Everstead Dispatch reconcile the current 8-vehicle Revision 3 into the final gate roster. Send one roster to Gate and Dispatch.\n06:15\nOPEN STAGING\nStaging/traffic officer opens the controlled lane; dispatcher opens the accountable event record.\n06:30-10:30\nSEQUENCED ARRIVALS\nGate verification, gate clearance, and command release occur as separate closed-loop decisions.\n1\nSTAGE\nEvery arrival goes to controlled staging. Dispatcher logs ARRIVED/STAGED. Mobile may show ACCEPTED; that does not permit entry.\n2\nVERIFY\nTwo gate verifiers positively match vehicle ID and driver record to the final current delivery record. Then report VERIFIED.\n3\nCLEAR GATE\nGate officer records GATE CLEARED only after VERIFIED. The vehicle may enter the controlled area; a mismatch never resolves at the roadway.\n4\nRELEASE MOVEMENT\nWatch Commander confirms required checks and the dispatch record, then authorizes RELEASED for escort / convoy movement.\nLate change rule\nA late manifest revision, unlisted vehicle, driver mismatch, or substitution does not inherit clearance. Gate officer keeps it in staging, dispatcher records HOLD, and the Watch Commander decides only after Everstead Dispatch provides a current record and the gate repeats positive verification.\nAuthority: MFAR 8.4, 8.7; CLPD GO-4.12; CD-2.7; Gate Post Order G-3\n7\n\n=== 幻灯片 8 ===\nTRANSITION AND EXCEPTIONS\nMixed mobile/radio operation on Thursday; defined fallback and safety-critical branch\n08\nTHURSDAY: TRANSITION MODE\n- Trained personnel may use mobile for receipt/assignment and status reporting.\n- Dispatcher mirrors mobile and radio actions in one accountable event record.\n- Personnel not trained on mobile use radio.\n- ACCEPTED stays dispatch acknowledgement only.\nRADIO FALLBACK\nTrigger: mobile outage, failed acknowledgement, device failure, or an untrained user.\n\nDispatcher declares RADIO FALLBACK, logs time/reason, and repeats closed-loop radio statuses. On restoration, dispatcher reconciles the radio record before resuming mobile.\nLATER: MOBILE STANDARD\nUpon formal CLPD activation, mobile becomes the primary dispatch workflow.\n\nRadio remains fallback. The same VERIFED, GATE CLEARED, RELEASED, HOLD, and supervisor controls do not change.\nMismatch / count / substitution branch\n1. Gate officer: HOLD in controlled staging; do not admit or reject based on a promise.\n2. Dispatcher: record HOLD and notify Watch Commander.\n3. Watch Commander: obtain current record from Everstead Dispatch and resolve the count/identity discrepancy.\n4. Gate: repeat positive vehicle/driver linkage. Only then VERIFIED -> GATE CLEARED -> RELEASED.\nSafety-critical interruption\nSpill, security threat, disabled vehicle, or comparable safety interruption: STOP routine movement; protect the gate/staging area; dispatcher notifies the Watch Commander; transfer to incident command under GO-4.12. Keep the emergency response separate from ordinary delay handling. Resume only on commander authorization.\nClosed-loop language prevents confusion: dispatch acknowledgement, verification, physical gate clearance, and movement release are different events with different owners.\nAuthority: CLPD Communications Directive CD-2.7; GO-4.12; Gate Post Order G-3\n8\n\n=== 幻灯片 9 ===\nAUTHORIZATION, CHANGE, AND CLOSEOUT\nThe Board authorizes one operation for Thursday and a controlled path to mobile standard\n09\nACTIVATION STATEMENT\nOn Board approval by 05:15 Thursday 23 Oct 2025, Jelena Skarsgard activates Plan A - Full-capacity hybrid - for the Everstead delivery. It replaces the interim radio-only / inconsistent mobile-pilot practice for this operation.\nWHAT CHANGES THURSDAY\n- Capacity and posts are set for the current eight-vehicle manifest.\n- 05:45 reconciliation creates one final gate roster.\n- Trained users may use mobile; dispatcher maintains radio continuity.\n- ACCEPTED cannot be treated as clearance.\n- Gate and movement release stay separate decisions.\nWHAT CHANGES AFTER MOBILE STANDARD\n- Mobile becomes primary only after formal CLPD activation.\n- Radio stays the declared fallback.\n- Dispatcher still maintains one accountable record.\n- Positive vehicle/driver linkage, HOLD, commander decision, GATE CLEARED, and RELEASED do not change.\nCLOSEOUT / ACCOUNTABILITY\nDispatcher closes and reconciles the dispatch event record; Gate 3 closes the final gate roster; Watch Commander documents any HOLD, exception, late change, or staffing change. Jelena Skarsgard owns the after-action review due Friday 24 Oct 2025 by 16:00.\nPacket refs: dated delivery brief; current transition terms; local authority snapshot\n9\n\n=== 幻灯片 10 ===\nEXECUTION CARD AND SOURCE TRAIL\nFour common situations have one unambiguous path\n10\nPlanned vehicle\nSTAGE -> ACCEPTED -> VERIFY -> GATE CLEARED -> RELEASED\nVehicle and driver match final roster; normal queue flow continues.\nAdditional verified vehicle\nUse Plan A eight-vehicle capacity; follow the same status sequence\nIt remains subject to final roster + positive link. Capacity is not automatic admission.\nUnlisted / mismatch\nHOLD in staging -> dispatcher log -> commander decision -> re-verify\nNo entry or release from an informal assurance, a prior list position, or Accepted.\nMobile outage\nDispatcher declares RADIO FALLBACK -> closed-loop radio -> reconcile\nUntrained personnel use the same radio path; resume mobile only after reconciliation.\nControlling record trail\nCapacity: Everstead client manifest Revision 3, received 15 Oct 2025 16:20.\nEntry: final 05:45 reconciled gate roster + gate positive verification.\nBaseline reference: CLPD logistics Revision 1, frozen 13 Oct 2025 14:00.\nMandatory authority trail\nMaryland Freight Facility Access Regulation (MFAR) 8.4, 8.6, 8.7; CLPD General Order GO-4.12; CLPD Communications Directive CD-2.7; Gate Post Order G-3. Frozen Maryland/local snapshot current as of 15 Oct 2025.\nSource packet only: manifest/logistics revisions, correspondence, Gate 3 exports, resources, and frozen authority snapshot\n10","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共10页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required file exists as a valid, openable PPTX at the specified filename.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"`everstead_delivery_operating_plan.pptx`","grader_hint":"Confirm that the top-level file has the exact filename, can be opened as a PowerPoint presentation, and is not merely a renamed non-PPTX file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation contains no more than 10 slides, counting title and source slides.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Presentation slide count","grader_hint":"Inspect the PPTX structure and count every slide, including hidden, title, appendix, and source slides."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The rendered presentation is legible and operationally usable, with no material clipping, overlap, unreadably small text, broken tables, or obscured decision content.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered slides","grader_hint":"Render all slides and review them at normal presentation size. Minor cosmetic imperfections are acceptable; defects that conceal facts, procedures, or staffing information are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation identifies the Thursday Everstead high-risk chemical delivery, the Cinderhall Logistics Board decision, and the operational audience.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Title, purpose, or opening decision frame","grader_hint":"Look for an unambiguous event and audience description rather than a generic convoy or dispatch presentation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation identifies Maryland as the jurisdiction and states that the governing snapshot is current as of 15 October 2025.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Decision frame, authority note, or source note","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent date formatting. Both the jurisdiction and as-of date must be visible in the deck."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation makes exactly one clear operating recommendation rather than presenting several co-equal choices or leaving the decision to the audience.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Executive decision statement and concluding content","grader_hint":"Identify the operative recommendation. Conditional details are acceptable, but there must be one selected operating model and no contradictory second recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is framed as the plan to be authorized by the Board for the delivery, not merely as an issue for later study.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Decision request or authorization statement","grader_hint":"Look for explicit approval or authorization language tied to the selected plan and delivery."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"At least two viable coverage and check-in alternatives from the supplied packet are compared before the recommendation is made.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Alternative comparison content","grader_hint":"Count substantively distinct alternatives. Renaming the same operating model does not create another alternative."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The alternatives are compared on their ability to cover the possible vehicle volume, including the disputed six-versus-eight range.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Alternative comparison and coverage rationale","grader_hint":"The comparison should explain operational capacity, not merely repeat alternative names or staffing totals."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The alternatives are compared on their check-in and verification model, including the practical role of radio and mobile dispatch.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Alternative comparison","grader_hint":"Look for meaningful distinctions in how arrivals are acknowledged, verified, and cleared under each alternative."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Resource demands shown for each compared alternative agree with the staffing facts and alternative definitions in the source packet.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Alternative staffing comparison","grader_hint":"Compare each displayed role count and total with the packet. Accept faithful aggregation, but not unsupported personnel, overtime, vehicles, or equipment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The alternatives are compared on safety and compliance risk rather than speed or convenience alone.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Alternative evaluation criteria and rationale","grader_hint":"Look for discussion of identity or vehicle mismatch, controlled entry, accountability, communications resilience, or equivalent supervisory risks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The alternatives are compared on continuity during the radio-to-mobile transition and suitability after mobile dispatch becomes standard.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Alternative comparison and operating horizon","grader_hint":"Both the Thursday transition and the later steady state must affect the comparison."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The comparison addresses service performance and gate delay without treating zero delay as an unconditional safety requirement.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Service criterion and recommendation rationale","grader_hint":"Credit a plan that preserves a credible flow or delay objective while retaining verification and exception controls."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The principal rejected alternative is rejected for a specific evidence-based tradeoff, not because it was simply not selected.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale","grader_hint":"The stated disadvantage should trace to packet evidence or a recognizable policing control concern and should distinguish the rejected option from the selected one."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation accurately identifies that the latest client manifest lists eight vehicles while the frozen police logistics sheet lists six.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Situation statement or source-reconciliation content","grader_hint":"Both counts and their source identities must be stated correctly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation explicitly chooses and names the source-precedence rule used to plan staffing for the disputed vehicle count.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Source-of-truth or planning-assumption statement","grader_hint":"Accept either defensible model supported by the packet: planning to the current eight-vehicle manifest, or using the frozen six-vehicle police sheet as the base with an executable verified surge condition. The choice must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The chosen precedence rule is justified using record currency, operational ownership, authorization status, and the consequences of under- or over-planning.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Source reconciliation rationale","grader_hint":"The rationale need not use every listed concept, but it must show deliberate supervisory reasoning rather than selecting the larger or smaller number without explanation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan distinguishes a record used for capacity planning from the verification authority required for an individual vehicle to enter or join the movement.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Source-precedence statement and operating procedure","grader_hint":"Credit either source-precedence model only if the deck makes clear that planning for a vehicle does not itself authorize that vehicle's entry."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck states that the informal no-delay assurance is not operational authority to waive verification, while preserving a reasonable service objective.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Decision rationale, boundary statement, or service standard","grader_hint":"Look for both parts: the assurance cannot override controls, and the plan still addresses orderly or timely processing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The common misuse of treating mobile “accepted” status as gate clearance is actively prohibited and replaced with a clear statement of what acceptance does and does not mean.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Mandatory control, status definitions, or quick-reference procedure","grader_hint":"Merely defining the status is insufficient. The deck should expressly say it is not entry clearance and direct personnel to the required verification or release step."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Period metrics do not recount duplicate operational events as separate check-ins.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Method note and displayed period metrics","grader_hint":"Recalculate or inspect the treatment of exact and obvious near-duplicate records using vehicle, checkpoint, time, and event context. The deck may use a defensible documented deduplication convention."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Duplicated or forwarded correspondence is not treated as independent corroboration, and the operative manifest or logistics revision used by the plan is identifiable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Source note or reconciliation rationale","grader_hint":"Look for version-aware use of the correspondence and records. A repeated email should not be cited as multiple confirmations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Malformed operational entries are either repaired from unambiguous context or excluded, with the treatment disclosed rather than silently converted into precise evidence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Data-treatment note and metrics","grader_hint":"Review the malformed timestamp or identifier records in the packet and confirm the deck's figures and caveat use a defensible, stated treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Training, demonstration, cancelled, or unrelated records are excluded from the operational period comparison.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period metric derivation or method note","grader_hint":"Check that rows visibly marked as non-operational or unrelated in the packet do not contribute to the reported delivery-operation metrics."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Inconsistent radio and mobile labels, status spellings, and equivalent vehicle identifiers are normalized consistently before comparison.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Method note and period categorization","grader_hint":"Inspect label variants in the packet. Equivalent labels must roll into the same category, while substantively different statuses must remain distinct."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The radio-baseline and mobile-pilot periods shown in the deck use the date boundaries supplied in the packet.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period comparison headings or notes","grader_hint":"Compare displayed period labels and included events with the packet's frozen period definitions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The reported check-in duration comparison is calculated from eligible cleaned events and uses the same metric definition for both periods.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period comparison metrics","grader_hint":"Verify that the comparison uses like-for-like start and completion events and that duplicates and ineligible rows are not counted. Accept a median or another defensible statistic if consistently defined."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The dispatch-acknowledgement comparison agrees with the eligible radio and mobile records after the deck's stated cleaning treatment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period comparison metrics","grader_hint":"Recalculate from the packet using the disclosed treatment. The displayed direction and figures must agree within ordinary rounding."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The exception, mismatch, hold, or rework comparison agrees with the eligible cleaned records and is not conflated with ordinary dispatch acceptance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period comparison metrics and status definitions","grader_hint":"Check the packet's exception statuses and confirm the deck counts operational exceptions consistently without classifying a routine accepted status as physical clearance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck explains the material period changes as a tradeoff: mobile dispatch improves at least one timeliness measure while transition issues increase or preserve a meaningful control or rework risk.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period-change narrative and recommendation rationale","grader_hint":"The explanation must reflect the packet's actual direction of change and avoid claiming that the pilot proves mobile-only operation is uniformly superior."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The period comparison includes an appropriate limitation concerning pilot scale, data quality, workflow differences, or the inability to infer full steady-state safety from the sample.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Data caveat or decision rationale","grader_hint":"Credit a specific limitation that affects interpretation; a generic disclaimer that all data may be imperfect is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected staffing level can support the plan's chosen vehicle-count assumption or its expressly defined surge condition.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Recommendation and staffing plan","grader_hint":"If the deck plans directly for eight, staffing must match the packet's corresponding capacity model. If it uses six as the base, the additional two must have a staffed, timed, and authorized surge path before admission."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The selected staffing plan assigns the necessary supervisory, gate-verification, staging or traffic, escort or response, and dispatch-coordination functions, allowing clearly explained compatible role combinations.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Staffing table and procedure ownership","grader_hint":"Review whether every essential function has an owner during the movement. Do not require a separate person for a function where the packet and procedure support a safe role combination."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Displayed staffing totals equal the sum of their component roles and agree everywhere the selected plan is summarized.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Staffing table, executive summary, and timeline","grader_hint":"Recalculate all selected-plan totals and compare repeated figures across slides. Conditional reserve personnel should be labeled consistently as deployed, standby, or contingent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected plan remains within the source packet's personnel, vehicle, dispatch, and overtime availability unless an identified approval condition is attached.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Staffing plan and assumptions","grader_hint":"Compare resource use with the packet. An option requiring additional approval is acceptable only when the approval, owner, and decision time are explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The staffing plan covers the full operational window and addresses relief, breaks, or loss of a key post without leaving verification or command unattended.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Staffing schedule or continuity note","grader_hint":"The solution may use rotation, relief, cross-coverage, or reserve arrangements, but must not assume uninterrupted performance by every post without explanation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The procedure includes a pre-arrival reconciliation point with a responsible owner, a decision time, and a defined treatment for late manifest or vehicle changes.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Pre-arrival procedure","grader_hint":"Look for an executable control before the delivery window. The precise format may vary, but late changes cannot simply inherit prior clearance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The arrival procedure directs vehicles into controlled staging or queuing so verification exceptions do not block the gate or force unsafe roadside handling.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Arrival flow or exception procedure","grader_hint":"Credit a clear safe location and traffic-control concept tied to ownership. Merely saying to avoid delays is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The procedure requires positive linkage of the arriving vehicle and driver to the authorized delivery record before gate entry or movement release.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Mandatory verification step","grader_hint":"Look for vehicle and driver checks against an authorized record, with shipment or movement linkage where supported. A mobile acknowledgement alone does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"An unlisted vehicle, identity mismatch, substitution, or unresolved count discrepancy triggers a hold and supervisor decision rather than automatic admission or rejection by an unsupported promise.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Exception and escalation path","grader_hint":"The deck should specify who holds the vehicle, who resolves the discrepancy, and what evidence or authorization is needed to proceed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The transitional communications procedure defines when mobile dispatch is used and provides a workable radio fallback for outage, failed acknowledgement, or untrained personnel.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Transition procedure and communications contingency","grader_hint":"A vague statement that radio remains available is insufficient. The fallback needs a trigger, ownership, and a way to preserve the operating record."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The procedure uses closed-loop, unambiguous status language so dispatch acknowledgement, verification, gate clearance, staging, and movement release cannot be confused.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Status definitions or communications protocol","grader_hint":"Look for distinct statuses and confirmation responsibility. Exact wording may vary if it is operationally clear and consistent with the packet."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Escort or convoy release occurs only after required checks and an identified supervisor or command role authorizes movement.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Release procedure and responsibility assignment","grader_hint":"The deck must distinguish arrival acknowledgement from authority to move and identify the role controlling release."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The procedure contains a concise branch for a spill, security threat, disabled vehicle, or other safety-critical interruption, including command notification and separation from routine delay handling.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Emergency or incident branch","grader_hint":"A full incident-response plan is not required. Credit a clear stop, protect, notify, and command-transfer concept appropriate to the packet's authorities."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan visibly distinguishes mandatory controls from preferred efficiency practices during the transition.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Procedure labels, control matrix, or quick-reference content","grader_hint":"Readers should be able to tell what cannot be waived from what may be adapted. Merely using the words “must” and “should” inconsistently does not earn credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Mandatory controls align with the packet's Maryland and local authority snapshot, while preferred practices do not dilute vehicle, driver, dispatch-record, or supervisory requirements.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Control classifications and authority references","grader_hint":"Compare the must/should boundary with the frozen extracts. In particular, the plan should not imply that escort status, a service assurance, or dispatch acceptance creates a legal waiver."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck cites or clearly attributes the controlling Maryland, local policy, and operating-record sources used for mandatory claims.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Source notes or citations","grader_hint":"Formal legal citation style is not required, but the cited identifiers or source titles must permit a reviewer to trace the rule within the packet."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan distinguishes what changes for Thursday from current radio practice and what changes later when mobile dispatch becomes the standard workflow.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Change summary, transition procedure, and steady-state procedure","grader_hint":"Both horizons must be concrete. A single future-state process that ignores the current transition does not earn credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck states the activation event or time, the authority that approves the plan, the operational owner, and what prior interim practice it replaces or modifies.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Authorization or implementation statement","grader_hint":"The statement should be sufficient to identify when the plan becomes operative and who controls it. It need not use formal general-order language."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The procedure includes post-operation accountability: closing dispatch and gate records, documenting exceptions or staffing changes, and assigning an after-action owner and deadline.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Closeout procedure","grader_hint":"Look for concrete record closure and review responsibility rather than a generic statement to conduct lessons learned someday."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A watch commander, dispatcher, or gate officer unfamiliar with the author's reasoning can execute the selected plan without asking which count, status, channel, role, or exception rule applies.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Presentation as a whole","grader_hint":"Walk through a normal six-vehicle arrival, an additional verified vehicle, an unlisted vehicle, and a mobile outage. Award credit only if the deck provides an unambiguous path for each."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Vehicle counts, staffing totals, role names, status terminology, timing, recommendation, and authority statements agree across the executive summary, comparison, staffing plan, and procedure.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Presentation as a whole","grader_hint":"Cross-check repeated facts within the PPTX. Minor harmless abbreviations are acceptable; contradictory counts, totals, decisions, or status meanings are not."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A first-line police supervisor in a Maryland public freight authority must turn conflicting stakeholder records and transition-period operating evidence into an authorized, executable plan for a single high-risk chemical delivery. The decision affects access control, officer safety, service reliability, legal compliance, and public confidence.","difficulty_sources":["Reconciling a current eight-vehicle client manifest with a frozen six-vehicle police logistics record without confusing planning precedence with admission authority","Balancing service continuity and delay reduction against non-waivable verification and supervisory safety controls","Comparing dirty radio-baseline and mobile-pilot records across periods without overstating a limited pilot","Designing one executable procedure for mixed radio/mobile operations and the later mobile-dispatch steady state","Keeping staffing figures, the selected vehicle-count model, status terminology, authorities, and procedural steps consistent within a compact presentation","Drawing and communicating a defensible must-versus-should boundary while actively correcting misuse of mobile acceptance as gate clearance"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_file":{"artifact":"Create one realistic DOCX source packet using only the entities and people named in the request and scenario directives.","content_requirements":["Include a dated delivery brief identifying the Thursday operating window, controlled location context, high-risk chemical nature of the movement, and the Board approval deadline.","Include a current client manifest revision showing eight vehicles and a separately frozen police logistics record showing six vehicles, with timestamps and ownership sufficient to support—but not dictate—a precedence decision.","Include correspondence containing the informal no-delay assurance, client expectations, dispatch concerns, gate concerns, and supervisory comments. Repeat one forwarded chain so a solver must not treat duplication as corroboration.","Include stakeholder notes from the permitted named people reflecting pressure for low delay, concern about unverified substitutions, transition training gaps, and the need for one operable Thursday plan.","Include radio-baseline and mobile-pilot event records plus concise contextual notes. The amount of data should support light extraction and calculation rather than extensive data engineering.","Include a resource and alternative summary with all staffing, vehicle, dispatch, scheduling, and approval facts needed to compare at least two viable models without inventing numbers.","Include a frozen Maryland and local-policy snapshot dated 15 October 2025 with traceable source titles or identifiers and enough quoted or accurately paraphrased authority for the required operational boundaries."]},"required_input_imperfections":["Place exact duplicate operational events in the period records.","Place at least one obvious near-duplicate using an equivalent vehicle identifier or status spelling.","Include a malformed timestamp or identifier whose treatment must be disclosed; make contextual repair defensible but not mandatory if exclusion is explained.","Include clearly marked training, demonstration, cancelled, or unrelated rows that should not contribute to operational metrics.","Use inconsistent but decipherable labels for radio, mobile, acknowledgement, verification, and selected vehicle identifiers.","Include a duplicated or forwarded email chain and more than one record revision so source currency matters.","Keep every defect discoverable without external research and avoid hidden metadata as the only evidence."],"period_relationships":["Define fixed, non-overlapping radio-baseline and mobile-pilot date boundaries in the packet.","After excluding ineligible rows, normalizing labels, and avoiding duplicate recounting, mobile dispatch must show a material improvement in at least one timeliness measure such as acknowledgement or completed check-in duration.","The mobile pilot must also show a meaningful countervailing issue, such as more mismatch holds, rework, failed acknowledgements, connectivity fallback, or confusion between accepted and cleared status.","Ensure the data do not support a claim that the mobile pilot is uniformly superior or ready for mobile-only operation without controls.","The gold calculations must use relational rules based on eligible cleaned events; displayed results may use ordinary presentation rounding."],"source_precedence_feasible_set":["Model A is defensible when the current eight-vehicle client manifest governs capacity planning, provided each vehicle still requires positive verification and the plan uses the packet's full-capacity staffing model.","Model B is defensible when the frozen six-vehicle police logistics record governs base deployment, provided the two additional vehicles cannot enter automatically and the plan includes the packet-supported reserve or surge trigger, decision time, owner, and verification path.","Neither model may treat the informal service assurance, mobile acceptance, planned capacity, or police escort as authority to waive gate verification or carrier obligations.","The reference solution should choose one model and remain consistent, while grader instructions must accept either model when fully executed."],"staffing_and_alternatives":["Supply at least one full-capacity hybrid option, one six-vehicle base option with a feasible conditional reserve or surge mechanism, and one lean mobile-dependent option whose apparent efficiency is offset by transition and control risk.","For each option, provide internally consistent personnel totals, role functions, timing, dispatch support, vehicle or equipment needs, and any overtime or approval condition.","Set available resources so at least two options are feasible but neither dominates on every criterion.","Ensure the selected-plan staffing can cover supervision, gate verification, safe staging or traffic, escort or response, dispatch coordination, and practical relief through the operating window.","Provide enough information to derive all staffing totals by summing component roles; do not require invented productivity ratios."],"authority_and_operating_boundaries":["The local status vocabulary in the packet must state that mobile “accepted” means dispatch receipt or assignment acknowledgement and is not physical gate clearance.","The frozen authority snapshot must support positive vehicle and driver linkage before controlled entry, supervisor resolution of unlisted or substituted vehicles, maintenance of an accountable dispatch record, and an incident-command response to a spill or safety threat.","The authority snapshot must make clear that escort status, emergency equipment, dispatch acceptance, or a client assurance does not create a waiver of access-control or hazardous-material obligations.","Mandatory controls should include verification before entry or release, controlled handling of mismatches, supervisor authority for exceptions, record accountability, and a usable communications fallback.","Preferred practices may include advance roster reconciliation, scheduled arrival spacing, mobile-first convenience once trained, and service-oriented queue management, provided they are not presented as substitutes for mandatory controls."],"procedure_expectations":["The gold plan must cover pre-arrival reconciliation, controlled arrival and staging, positive verification, discrepancy hold and escalation, dispatch status handling, movement release, mobile outage fallback, safety-critical interruption, and post-operation closeout.","The Thursday procedure must work with mixed radio and mobile capability; the steady-state procedure may make mobile primary but must retain a defined continuity path.","Activation must be tied to Board approval and the source packet's delivery timing, with a named operational role responsible for implementation and control of subsequent changes.","The closeout must reconcile gate and dispatch records, preserve exception and staffing-change documentation, and assign a timely after-action review."],"reference_solution_validation":["Build an offline reference PPTX with no more than 10 slides and render it to confirm readability.","Recalculate all period metrics from the packet after the documented cleaning treatment.","Verify every selected-plan staffing total against component roles and resource availability.","Walk the reference procedure through a normal planned arrival, an additional verified vehicle, an unlisted vehicle, a mobile outage, and a safety-critical interruption.","Confirm that recommendation, counts, staffing, status meanings, authority, timing, and change statements agree throughout the deck.","Confirm that all rubric items can be satisfied from the supplied packet, occupationally standard supervisory judgment, and the submitted PPTX without internet access."]},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"everstead_delivery_source_packet","filename":"everstead_delivery_source_packet.docx","purpose":"Provides the correspondence, stakeholder notes, conflicting vehicle records, radio-baseline and mobile-pilot operating data, staffing alternatives, delivery constraints, and frozen Maryland legal and policy snapshot needed for the operating decision.","required":true,"discovery":"Read the complete packet before preparing the operating plan; relevant evidence appears in both narrative records and embedded operational tables."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"everstead_delivery_operating_plan","filename":"everstead_delivery_operating_plan.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"Board-ready operating-plan presentation containing the decision, alternative comparison, staffing plan, transitional and steady-state procedure, controls, and execution responsibilities for the delivery.","required":true,"constraints":["Place the file at the top level of the working directory.","Use exactly the required filename.","Use no more than 10 slides, including title and source material.","Make exactly one operating recommendation.","Use only factual figures supported by the supplied packet.","Cover both Thursday's transitional operation and the mobile-dispatch steady state."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":6.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution should be drafted from the frozen packet using reproducible calculations, then reviewed independently by a first-line law-enforcement operations reviewer and a second reviewer for data reconciliation, presentation usability, and internal consistency. Reviewers must accept either of the two defensible source-precedence models when it is explicit, fully staffed, and preserves verification authority.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The operational records are synthetic and demonstrate a transition-period decision rather than estimating performance for all future deliveries.","The frozen legal and policy extracts support only the stated as-of date and should not be treated as a live legal update.","Two source-precedence models are intentionally acceptable, so grading must evaluate coherence and execution rather than require one literal recommendation."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:34:33+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:34:33+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:34:33+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:34:33+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/06cfeb79b330c212","declared_deliverables":["everstead_delivery_operating_plan.pptx"]};
